\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{Response tasks and interactive tasks}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{evaluation}

\p{A task and a task law specify admissible instance--outcome pairs and how
instances are sampled. Benchmark datasets usually present an input and expect an answer in a
parseable format. Reinforcement-learning formulations instead name states,
actions, and reward; see \citet{Chapter 3}{sutton2018reinforcement}. The
admissibility predicate captures parseability, while the law records how
instances are sampled. Neither choice already identifies parsing with success.}

\p{One-shot inference and an interactive environment can share the same task
interface. Claims about an interaction's temporal structure additionally
depend on the environment and the observations available at each decision.}
